<BaseLayout title="Editorial Methodology" description="How Creator Growth Tools researches SaaS comparisons and reviews.">
  <section class="page">
    <p class="eyebrow">Creator Growth Tools Editorial</p>
    <h1>Editorial Methodology</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Creator Growth Tools publishes research-backed SaaS comparisons for creators, newsletter operators, solopreneurs, and small online businesses.</p>

      <h2>Evidence hierarchy</h2>
      <p>Official vendor pricing, product, help, affiliate, and legal pages are used for facts such as plan names, feature limits, pricing examples, and program terms.</p>
      <p>Verified review marketplaces such as G2, Capterra, or similar sources are used to identify recurring user praise and complaints. We treat repeated patterns as stronger evidence than isolated comments.</p>
      <p>Reddit and other community discussions are anecdotal. They can reveal useful workflow language or friction points, but one discussion is not treated as proof of broad platform quality.</p>

      <h2>No fake hands-on claims</h2>
      <p>We do not claim to have tested or personally used a product unless an actual hands-on test occurred. When an article is based on official documentation and public review consensus, it says so.</p>

      <h2>Affiliate neutrality</h2>
      <p>Affiliate relationships do not determine recommendations. Articles may include non-affiliate tools when they are useful to the reader, and commercial articles include a visible affiliate disclosure.</p>

      <h2>Verification dates</h2>
      <p>Facts are timestamped with a last verified date and should be refreshed when pricing, plan structure, affiliate terms, or public review consensus changes materially.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
